# 13. SECURITY AND PRIVACY

Apply appropriate security to every new feature.

### Authentication and authorization

- Reuse the existing authentication system.
- Enforce the actual customer, artisan, and administrator roles.
- Verify product and profile ownership on the server.
- Protect private buyer enquiries and business analytics.
- Use least-privilege access.
- Preserve and validate database access policies.

### AI and uploads

- Call privileged AI providers from secure server-side code.
- Keep API keys in environment variables or the deployment's secret manager.
- Validate file types, file sizes, and upload permissions.
- Validate AI outputs against structured schemas.
- Treat user uploads and generated text as untrusted input.
- Handle provider failures, timeouts, and rate limits.
- Avoid storing voice recordings unnecessarily.
- Obtain appropriate consent for recording and processing audio.
- Explain external processing where relevant.

### Data integrity

- Preserve referential integrity.
- Use safe migrations and appropriate constraints.
- Prevent duplicate submissions.
- Use transactional operations when consistency requires them.
- Do not overwrite production data with sample data.
- Keep test data clearly separated from real data.

### Payment safety

Preserve the existing checkout architecture.

Distinguish simulated payment from real payment processing. Never mark an order as paid solely because the browser reports success.

